From P?S8 Google Group<br><br><div class="gmail_quote"><div dir="auto" class="gmail_attr">---------- Forwarded message ---------<br>From: <span dir="auto">cjl &lt;Unknown&gt;</span><br>Date: Thursday, August 3, 2017 at 3:10:48 AM UTC-5<br>Subject: P?S/8 system device handler [and related] [NO RL!]<br>To: <span dir="auto">P?S/8 &lt;Unknown&gt;</span><br></div><br><br><html-blob><div dir="ltr">Hopefully accurate, this is a list of all known programming starts for P?S/8 system device handler support [and related issues where relevant[.<br><br>The info is an attached file to preserve formatting, but is otherwise just an ordinary text file.<div><br></div><div>Feel free to comment or question anything, etc.</div><div><br></div><div>cjl</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">P?S/8 Storage Device Status a/o 03-Aug-2017

This is the present status of the storage devices supported by P?S/8 [past, present
and future] with appropriate commentary on each configuration.

Note: In some cases, non-system handlers were never written for certain devices.
This is not a serious problem as non-system handlers are allowed far more code space
than a typical system handler occupies [and actually has less to accomplish].
Moreover, all non-system handlers will be modified to use the new double-precision
block argument form required for use with the P?S/8 SHELL for future release.  [At
present, only P?S/8 BLKCPY uses the non-system handlers in their present form and
implementation.  BLKCPY will require internal modification to upgrade to eight-digit
octal block number support over the present four-digit functionality.]

Note: All devices are defined in designated file names.  Those indicated with * are
not presently available.  All others should be capable of creating a P?S/8 starter
system by combining with the other assembled binaries of a P?S/8 starter system.
Once booted, additional procedures are required to finish a complee release that are
for the most part handler-independent; in a few cases, certain programs are
recommended for inclusion because they are often found on the systems the release is
associated with [althought not necessarily].  Anyone with proper knowledge of the
P?S/8 system programs directory can customize it accordingly.  Depending on the
specifics, the TFS and extended file directory limit block can also be defined, etc.


PQSDTA.PAL

This is the P?S/8 system handler complex file for the TC01/TC08 DECtape controller
with up to eight DECtape drives supported directly as logical units.  This is the
foundational system device; all system kernel design is based on the limitations of
this device [which can run on a 4K system].

All device-specific features for any P?S/8 system are contained in such a file as
this:

a) The actual system device handler [which eventually loads into 07600-07777].  For
   a device requiring extended memory [not applicable to TC01/TC08] eight additional
   pages of memory are allocated in the highest extended memory field at x6000
   through x7777 where x is the highest field of memory; ten blocks are allocated on
   the system device to support this configuration [including two blocks used to
   make the system operational; this is necessary due to some of the more quirky
   bootup conventions of certain devices, coupled with the special needs associated
   with virtually all of these handlers].

b) The Control-S/Control-Q routine in the keyboard monitor.  Implementation varies,
   but the TC01/TC08 DECtape implementation is the standard; pressing Control-R
   during the keyboard entry of any command rewinds the current logical unit DECtape
   drive overlapping the further entry of the command.  This can be used to
   advantage to speed various operations such as entry of an execution-class
   command; the system program directory and other system components are near the
   beginning of the DECtape, while often the tape is positioned just past the latest
   file [having just been saved from the contents of the edit buffer].  [Note: Due
   to the 100% caching of the Tiny File System [TFS] directory, once a file is
   entered into the directory, file edits only update the file blocks; the directory
   is never referenced unless and until further directory change is made.  A typical
   scenario is a file is being edited, saved and then an assembly will be initiated.
   Pressing Control-R during the command entry can significantly shorten execution
   time.

   Pressing Control-T will rewind and unload the current logical unit DECtape drive.
   If the DECtape drive is a type TU55, the supply reel brakes will lock to aid in
   removal of the DECtape media [without having to place the drive off-line or
   selection of an alternate unit].  Due to hardware limitations, a type TU56 drive
   will ignore the final command.  After the last command is sent to the controller,
   the keyboard monitor will allow further command input.

   The implementation of this set of functions varies with the device; in general,
   the functionality is inferior to the TC01/TC08 implementation due to either
   hardware restrictions or lack of code space in the keyboard monitor or both.
   Storage devices that require an extended-memory device handler extension may
   implement further functionality within the device extension where applicable as
   code space availability is generally not an issue.

   For devices where there is less opportunity for performance improvment, the
   implementation may be non-existent or performs only some modest improvement such
   as recalibrating a diskette drive, etc.

c) Interception routines for use with the Logical Console Overlay.  This is a
   section of patches to be performed by the Logical Console Overlay when it is
   initialized for the purposes of improving the performance of the system device
   handler.  Common routines generally implemented are an intercept for the system
   device handler HALT to allow user interaction with the cause of the error [and
   perhaps recovering from the error if possible].  This generally requires
   replacing the HLT instruction and the instruction following [or equivalent] to a
   CIF x0 instruction [where x is the field where the Overlay resides, generally the
   highest field of extended memory], followed by a JMP yyy where yyy is a
   designated location on page zero of the Overlay.  Invariably, there is also
   another intercept to setup the latest call parameters to assist in the most
   verbose form of the error interception [which can give complete information about
   the latest call].

   Additional intercepts may be implemented to improve handler performance that led
   to compromise to allow the handler to function in a 4K environment.  [Generally,
   handlers that require an extended-memory device extension are capable of all
   operations at maximum efficiency and effectiveness.]  In the case of the
   TC01/TC08 system device handler there are several improvements made:

   i)  To protect against attempts at impossible block transfers, a retry count is
       implemented to prevent infinite loops attempting to search for excessively
       high block numbers.  This improvement must be implemented in this manner
       because multiple tape lengths are supported; a static determination is not
       feasible.

   ii) The ability to reckon the correct initial search direction for the latest
       handler call is hampered in that there is no accounting for change of logical
       device unit; this is generally not a serious limitation.  To prevent this
       from happening, a table of last blocks transferred is created for all eight
       DECtape drives; the corresponding unit is used for the latest calculation
       which makes all calculations perfectly accurate.

d) Slurp binary loader.  Where implemented, the slurp loader can provide impressive
   performance improvements during binary file loading, including the ability to
   load memory with the system device write-protected.  [When the slurp loader is
   not used, a virtual loader is used which requires writing on an a set of system
   scratch blocks reserved for the purpose of virtualizing  all affected areas of
   memory; after all files are otherwise loaded, the entire virtual area is brought
   into memory from the virtual buffer.  By nature, this is a slower process as well
   as requiring write-enabling the system device.

   Not every device can support a slurp loader; on such system variants, the virtual
   loader is always used.  In general, of the original three-cycle data break
   devices, the TC01/TC08 and the DF32 are capable of supporting a slurp loader.
   Due to very fast DMA loading speed, the RF08 is too fast to support a slurp
   loader [but is otherwise so fast the virtual loader is more than adequate].  All
   single cycle DMA devices cannot support a slurp loader; in general, all program
   transfer devices should be capable of supporting a slurp loader.  Performance can
   be tested to observe the performance improvement of using a slurp loader where
   both variants are available.

   In a notable "demo" given by Charles Lasner of the P?S/8 slurp loader versus the
   mediocre virtual loader of OS/8 [ABSLDR], P?S/8 running on TC08 DECtapes actually
   loaded large binary files **FASTER** than OS/8 performing the same task using a
   pair of RK8E/RK05 drives!  [Note: While not performed at that time, a direct
   comparison of P?S/8 virtual slurp loading on the RK8E configuration would also be
   faster; this is because slurp format is inherently more efficient than OS/8
   binary files because the format used is inherently inefficient for no valid
   reason.  How OS/8 came to use a poor choice of binary formats is the subject of
   another document.  The simplest answer is this was done as a direct result of
   ignorant management interfering with the design, something pervasive throughout
   OS/8.  It is theorized that had DEC management been more reasonable, OS/8 would
   never have existed; instead P?S/8 including the optional SHELL overlay would have
   been the proper system.  While this is all theoretical: a) P?S/8 is based on the
   forerunner R-L Monitor System.  The R-L Monitor System was rejected by these
   incompetent managers; some years later, P?S/8 was demonstrated to DEC management.
   P?S/8 was rejected as well - twice!  After most of the original pioneering PDP-8
   programmers rejected working on OS/8 as defined from a hasty demo version created
   in 48 hours over a weekend, I have the dubious distinction of being the fifth
   person to reject this as well; instead I and others developed the R-L Monitor
   System into P?S/8 as it became known a few years later.  The demo mentioned above
   was quite impressive to PDP-8 programmers present at the time; the manager
   deliberately ignored the demonstration in a misguided attempt at a "power play"
   over his staff instead of what would be best for the company.  The first time
   P?S/8 was demonstrated, the then-current manager rejected it for a preposterous
   notion: He only wanted to know what was the **MINIMUM** memory configuration that
   P?S/8 could be made to run in; I honestly answered 4K [thinking this was a good
   point to make, and did not mention that 8K is needed for the RK8E and other
   configurations].  The manager rejected P?S/8 solely on the notion that this
   "could lead to less memory sales".  What I was not aware of is that this same
   dim bulb was part of a cabal of managers who had originally demanded that OS/8
   **REQUIRE** 8K memory minimum to encourage larger CPU configurations [and thus
   profit].  Part of the mediocrity in the design of OS/8 is a needless requirement
   for 8K for all configurations.  The design of the P?S/8 optional SHELL
   environment is such that many functions can be performed in 4K; however much
   more memory is **HIGHLY RECOMMENDED** to improve and extend performance.

   Hypothetically, had I been there to first propose a long-term project to develop
   a proper operating system, meaningful dialogue might have been possible other
   than a pathetically short-sighted interest in initial memory sales.  The SHELL
   represents a better environment than OS/8 does, including support for slightly
   modified versions of all important OS/8 system programs.  With proper design,
   nearly all programs can benefit from as much extended memory as is available up
   to 32K where available; 4K may be adequate for even some of these OS/8 programs
   that can run in 4K if the operating system is capable of supporting the program
   in 4K.  Reasonable performance probably requires 16K in general; however, all
   available memory is dynamically allocated.  As such, as much performance is
   obtained as possible.

   Note: By way of example, P?S/8 PAL as of the present implementation can take
   advantage of as much as 20K memory; the operating system may raise that
   requirement to 24K.  A future XPAL implementation will use even more memory.]

   The begining of the slurp loader that loads into 07600-07642 [through 07641 in
   the next release] also functions as a stand-alone complete P?S/8 reboot routine
   for the present configuration.  In many cases, other operating systems may also
   be compatible with this reboot program.  In certain cases, this code in theory
   could be somewhat smaller, but the main emphasis is implementing the slurp loader
   correctly.

   The P?S/8 BIN program supports a command-line option to load into the last page
   of field 0 the stand-alone bootstrap mentioned above along with a P?S/8 version
   of the paper-tape BIN loader and the standard RIM loader.  Besides code
   optimization, the stand-alone feature of reader device choice [high or low-speed
   reader as chosen by the front panel switch register] has been removed.
   Additional command-line option switches determine the choice of high or low-speed
   reader that applies to both the BIN and RIM loaders; which reader is started is
   chosen by another command-line option.  The removed feature is superfluous in an
   environment launched from a storage-based operating system.  Additionally, as of
   the next release [which imposes the one-shorter overall length requirement of the
   stand-alone bootstrap], the P?S/8 implementation of the BIN loader will be the
   only known version that cannot be destroyed by loading and execution programs for
   any of the three-cycle data break devices.  All other known implementations can
   generally be destroyed if programs are executed for the DF32 or RF08 yet are not
   affected by programs that access the TC58 magnetic tape controller [which is
   seldom used].  The present P?S/8 BIN loader can be destroyed only by programs for
   the TC58 magnetic tape controller; in the next release this restriction will be
   removed [by taking advantage of the additional word freed up from the present
   definition].  [Note: It appears that the specification is two words longer than
   the longest known implementation, which is the TC01/TC08 implementation as of
   Version 8Z.  All other devices can be made to conform to this slightly more
   demanding specification requiring little to no modifications of the handler for
   future release.]  The source code of all handlers will be updated to reflect the
   new requirement of the next release.

e) System device reloader.  This is a counter-intuitive extension feature of the
   slurp loader.

   When this option is not invoked ["/I" command switch to P?S/8 BIN] as many as 17
   slurp  binary files can be passed to the slurp loader for loading into memory.
   Once the loading is finished, the program is in memory; however, the system
   device handler is not present [although the system can be rebooted by starting at
   07600].

   In general, 16 binary files is adequate even if loading a fairly long program,
   around 24K of memory being loaded in a single BIN command.  [If the binary were
   actually longer, the loading can be split into two commands.]  [Note: When P?S/8
   BINCON is implemented, it will be possible to combine as many as 17 binary files
   into a single extended-length file.  By the use of several instances of the
   BINCON program including an existing extended-length slurp binary format
   extended-length file, it is possible to create a single file that can load the
   entirety of 32K with only one file specification passed to the BIN program.]

   When a system supports a slurp loader, the virtual loader becomes an option
   instead of the default.  In that case, the full 17 files can be supported for a
   single BIN command.  When the slurp loader is available and the "/I" command-line
   switch is invoked, the maximum list size is lowered to 16.

   The reason for this is that an internal "file" is appended to the end of the list
   of passed files.  This "file" can be likened to a "benevolent virus" as it
   selectively destroys the slurp loader code performing the loading operation.  The
   are reserverd for input files passed to every system program [including BIN] is
   used to load 17 additional code words.  Eventually, the slurp loader is rigged to
   execute this code just read in a few cycles earlier.

   The code now in control modifies the present contents of memory to become a near
   bootup routine; the difference is that the code remains in control until the
   very last instance, at which point the normal starting field and address words
   are  branched to for the purpose of starting up the overall program being loaded.
   The difference is that now the system device handler has actually been fully
   reloaded and can be called; effectively, the environment described when the
   virtual loader would be used is now completely available.

   The "file" that accomplishes this is hardwired to be the contents of block 0060
   on the system device.  As such, not only is this code easily available [and not
   actually a file in the directory], it is also located close to the beginning of
   the device making the reload of the system device handler a quick operation.

   System programs are started up by an embedded call to the system device handler
   followed by a JMP I .+1 followed by the system program starting address.

   The default contents of these words [just reloaded] is a dedicated call to write
   all of field 0 to the system field 0 virtual scratch area defined as blocks 0020
   through 0057.  [Note: These blocks have unrelated meaning including quick
   temporary files known as % and $.  The variant groups are incompatible with each
   other; the usage must be kept to only members of one particular grouping.  The
   one illustrated here is when the dedicated field 0 core image is in effect.
   Programs such as ODT, START and BSAVE depend upon this usage.  It is possible to
   defer debugging or other operations on the core image by saving the % and $ files
   to named TFS files and later reversing the process, etc.]

   By specifying the "/I" command-line option switch and =7632 to BIN, all of the
   above operations can be achieved in one command.  As a convenience to the user,
   the alias to the BIN program known as GET sets these parameters making command
   invocation even easier.  [Note: To specify an extended memory starting field, the
   command-line switches "/1" through "/7" can be used.  In this circumstance, none
   of these switches can be set.  Future releases of the GET program will force
   these command-line switches off before the BIN program is started.  [Note: GET is
   actually an alternate entry point into the BIN program expressly for this
   purpose.  This safety feature is expected to be implemented in P?S/8 Version 9A.]

   Note: The source code of a device handler has to create an image of block 0060 in
   slurp binary format.  Advanced assembly methods are used to accomplish this while
   still maintaining general readability.

Notable features:

1) The handler automatically determines the correct initial search direction based
   on the last block transferred via the previous handler call.

2) Virtually all other systems for TC01/TC08 DECtape are designed to bootup on drive
   0.  P?S/8 for TC01/TC08 defines an extended bootstrap that can bring the system
   up on any logical unit 0 through 7 [Nearly all systems can be booted with the
   extended bootstrap code as long as unit 0 is set in the front panel switches
   where the unit is read from bits[0-2].

PQSRKA.PAL

This is the handler complex file for P?S/8 using the RK8E [or RK8F] disk controller
with a variety of disk cartridges configurations:

a) Logical units 0 through 7 are on a single RK05 drive.  Each logical unit is 3130
   octal [1624 decimal] blocks [which compares favorably with the typical size of
   DECtape or LINCtape].

   This system can be booted on any RK05 drive 0-3.  Note: Future RK8E support may
   include support for the RKS8E which allows up to eight drives including drives
   4-7.  An extended bootstrap will be required to access drives 4 through 7.  There
   are two standard bootstraps for the RK8E, one is for drive unit 0 only and is
   only two instructions long.  The other somewhat longer bootstrap allows booting
   to any of the drives 0 through 3.

b) Logical units 0 through 3 are on one drive and logical units 4 through 7 are on
   the other drive in an even/odd drive pair.  Each logical unit is 6260 octal [3248
   decimal] blocks, which is exactly half of the size of an OS/8 logical device
   [RKAx: or RKBx:].  Note: P?S/8 OS8CON [and the future utility BINCON under
   development] require all data to be converted between systems to be in the first
   half of the OS/8 device.  This configuration allows even logical units to map
   properly for this requirement.

   Most of the same considerations apply to this configuration variant.  The
   standard extended bootstrap can be used to bootup this system.  If drive 0 or
   drive 2 are booted to, then logical unit 4 through 7 are available on drive 1
   or drive 3 respectively.  If drive 1 or drive 3 are booted to, then logical unit
   0 through 3 are available on drive 0 or 2 respectively.  Thus, the scope of this
   system is limited to a pair of drives.  [Note: P?S/8 does not require the boot
   drive be zero.  However, there is a temporary restriction on the unfinished
   cross-reference option of the P?S/8 PAL assembler.  If the "/X" option is used,
   references are written "raw" to logical unit 7; additionally, there is presently
   no sorted listing output of the references [although they have been confirmed to
   be valid.  This restriction will be lifted in the next release when this portion
   of P?S/8 PAL is rewritten to output to a variable list of extended-length output
   files that can be configured for this purpose with the P?S/8 SET command.  This
   will allow complete flexibility, as well as the creation of far longer reference
   output for truly humongous assemblies.  Please note that P?S/8 PAL, in a manner
   similar to TOPS-10 PAL10, outputs actual statement numbers with actual references
   while PAL8 creates a listing file; OS/8 CREF then reads the listing file and
   creates **PRESUMPTIVE** cross-reference output!  By using various features that
   are part of the standard language implemented in all three assemblers, it is
   trivial to hide statement numbers, or create output that is actually a comment
   that foolishly OS/8 CREF will attempt to cross-reference!  Since CREF is also
   a limited program in many other ways, this could prevent CREF from finishing the
   cross-reference and/or require certain options to curtail some of the features
   such as cross-referencing literals.  Even accommodating the contrived "mammoth"
   option of CREF, it is incapable of fully cross-referencing many programs, and
   ironically in particular, the assembly of the P?S/8 PAL assembler itself!

   There exists a known "sabotege" file from DEC [the source code file of FOCAL-8
   which is not productively better than the source code for FOCAL, 1969] designed
   to make FOCAL-8 seem to fail due to hardware problems on the competitor's
   product, the DCC-112 and/or DCC-112H.  The product release for FOCAL-8 is only
   associated with a listing created by PAL8 and CREF that completely hides the
   treachery by deliberate obfuscation.  Alternate words that are innocuous are
   deliberately shown in the listing; only a direct load of the binary reveals the
   true nature of the binary.  Using either OS/8 BITMAP or P?S/8 MAP reveals the
   affected locations are double-loaded and the unseen instructions are the ones
   loaded into memory.  Had this listing been created using either TOPS-10 PAL10 or
   P?S/8 PAL, the gap in the statement numbering would reveal some aspects of the
   treachery.

c) Logical units 0 and 1 are on drive 0, logical units 2 and 3 are on drive 1,
   logical units 4 and 5 are on drive 2 and logical units 6 and 7 are on drive 3.

   The scope of this system is identical to the usual OS/8 allocation on RK05 packs
   with each P?S/8 logical unit corresponding to RKAx: and RKBx: on any drive.  It
   is conceivable with proper RKS8E discovery, all access could translate to drives
   4 through 7, etc.

   Each P?S/8 logical unit is 14540 octal [6496 decimal] blocks which corresponds to
   6260 octal [3248 decimal] OS/8 records.  The P?S/8 system device handler cannot
   access blocks larger than 7777 octal [4095 decimal].  In the next release, P?S/8
   will support non-system handlers with double-precision arguments.  When the
   corresponding non-system handler is available, the entire block structure on all
   8 drives will be available from one handler.  As such, P?S/8 in this 
   configuration has storage scope parity with OS/8.

d) Logical unit 0 is the entirety of drive 0 through logical unit 3 is the entirety
   of drive 3.  If RKS8E support can be added, this will extend to logical unit
   4 is the entirety of drive 4 through logical unit 7 is the entirety of drive 7.
   iN THIS configuration P?S/8 is superior to OS/8 since there are 31300 octal
   [12992 decimal] blocks per logical unit.  As in d) above, the system device
   handler can only access up to block 7777 [4095 decimal].  In the next release,
   P?S/8 will support non-system handlers with double-precision arguments.  When the
   corresponding non-system handler is available, the entire block structure on all
   drives will be available from one handler.  As such, P?S/8 in this configuration
   has superior storage access than OS/8. [Note: This mode has not been written yet,
   but is a fairly trivial change to the source code of both the P?S/8 system device
   handler and the corresponding existent non-system handler source file.]

   All P?S/8 RK8E/RK8F [and eventually RKS8E] handlers address the physical storage
   to the nearest one/half logical block of 128 words.  This is accomplished by
   having a two-page buffer within each handler.  The worst-case user call to the
   handler can be broken down as follows:

   i)   The call could consist of accessing the second half of a physical record
        without referencing the first half of the same physical record.  If the
        requested operation is a read, the entire physical record is read into the
        buffer within the handler, then the second half of the record is copied by
        a move through memory routine into the corresponding portion of the caller's
        input buffer.

        If the requested operation is a write, the entire record is first read into
        the internal buffer within the handler; then the caller's data is copied
        from the caller's buffer into the second half of the buffer within the
        handler, then the physical record is written out.

   ii)  This portion of the call could consist of one or more aligned double-page
        block pairs corresponding to a physical RK05 record.  Reads and writes to
        these records are done conventionally without using the internal buffer
        within the handler.  Statistically, most calls consist of this form, and
        only occasionally the potential portion described in iii) below.  As such,
        throughput using the P?S/8 RK05-series handlers is quite good.

   iii) This portion of the call could consist of accessing only the first half of
        the physical record.  If the current call is a write, the entire physical
        record is read into the internal buffer, then the caller's data is copied
        to the first page of the internal buffer.  Then the physical record is
        written out.  If this portion of the call is a read operation, it is
        performed directly using the RK hardware feature of "first half only" [which
        checksums the second half without DMA transfer].  While this feature is
        available in the RK controller for writing, this is not used as this would
        destroy the second half of the record by replacing the data with 0000.

The P?S/8 handler for the RK controller requires an extended-memory device handler
extension.  There is no support for Control-S/Control-Q or the slurp loader [or the
slurp loader reload function].  Attempts at passing the "/I" switch to P?S/8 BIN are
ignored and the maximum of 17 files passed is maintained.  Future releases of P?S/8
may issue a warning message if the "/I" switch is passed to indicate it is being
ignored.

The Logical Console Overlay interception routines allow for elegant recovery from
errors without using the wrong methods that do not apply to the current problem.  In
a somewhat famous incident, I was able to recover the entire contents of specific
RK05 cartridges after the drive heads were aligned; the data had been written prior
to the field service maintenance.  P?S/8 BLKCPY is prepared to intensely retry a
single logical block if need be after a larger buffer call returns an error.  By
avoiding the foolish recalibrate operations of OS/8, the drive power supply was not
repeatedly thrown out of voltage adjustment allowing the [eventual] complete
recovery of all the local users' RK05 cartridges [after repeated attempts at trying
to use OS/8 had miserably failed].  [Note: Some thanked me and some were in total
disbelief but could not argue with success.]

PQSRXA.PAL

This system handler is designed for the RX01 only.  It complete supports all known
variations on the RX01 design including the RX02/RX03 with single-density media
inserted, including all of the logical unit extensions implemented in the VT78,
DECmate I, DECmate II with the RX278 option, the DSD-210 and the DSD-440 which use
different addressing schemes to access additional drives past the first drive pair.
[The VT78 and DECmate I use the same extension to achieve a total of four drive
units while the RX278 uses an incompatible scheme that requires support for an
additional drive select mechanism unique to this option; the handler will not
erroneously access the RX50 drive[s] on the DECmate II.]  The DSD-240 supports up to
four drives [a special front panel is required to access the first three drives; the
standard front panel only has the first two drives.  The disk bus is accessible from
within the DSD-210 chassis to add on external drive units, etc.  [Note: The DSD-210
can format single-density media in the real sense.  OS/8 supports a program that is
erroneously named "FORMAT" which is actually just a media density change program; it
cannot operate on damaged header media, which is a condition generally repairable
using the DSD-210 as long as there is no actual physical damage to the media.]

All forms of write-protect are properly supported [including user modification of
the DEC RX01, which was designed to support write-protect, but DEC removed key
components that can be restored].  The RX02 drive is properly supported, even if
the RX01-compatibility switch within the drive is thrown.  [Note: Because of
wide-spread bad documentation, many programs fail on a write-protected drive because
of claimed anomaly with the RX02.  By ignoring the RX02 status and interrogating the
drive in alternate ways, the proper current status can be achieved.

RX03 double-sided disks are supported; the only known production was for in-house
prototypes within DEC that were used to design handlers for OS/8 and also RT-11.
This P?S/8 handler is fully compatible with the RX03 specification, but only for
single-density media.  Most notably, the presence of double-sided media will not
be misinterpreted as a drive-based error.  Note: DSD also produced
an RX03-compatible drive; however, it was integrated into a Q-bus controller and
not made available for any PDP-8 model.  If someone wants to implement a modern
replacement for this drive, the proper specifications are available.  Since the
RX series of drives is quite small, it is highly recommended the full RX03 be
implemented.

This handler uses superior track and sector calculation using a two-stage method.
The initial sector of a transfer is calculated using a divide by 13 routine derived
from one of the EAE simulators.  Subsequent track and sector information is obtained
from a very short code section using predictor/corrector techniques.  As a result,
while OS/8 takes 13 times as would be typical on a PDP-8/E on a VT78 [due to failing
to keep up with 2:1 interleave due to the slow CPU coupled with a very poor
algorithm to perform the divide by 13 using repetitive subtraction], this handler
can keep up with the intended 2:1 interleave on that significantly slower model.
[Note: It is likely that even the PDP-8/E falls behind when accessing the higher
tracks on the drive!  On the VT78, the performance degradation comes into play on
attempts to access much lower tracks.]

Flawless error recovery is performed by this handler; only the correct recovery
method is used to allow maximum available throughput even if errors occur.  [In the
OS/8 handlers, there is insufficient space to do a proper error analysis.  As such,
all error methods are always applied for any error situation.  Most notably, a
drive recalibration that takes several seconds occurs on such as read parity errors
for which the proper procedure should be to merely retry the read operation.]

The corresponding non-system handler is based on this system device handler.  As
such, it cannot handle double-density media.  Future releases of P?S/8 will include
an alternate system device handler that only handles RX02/03 drives with a
double-density diskette inserted [which can optionally be double-sided].

The corresponding non-system handler will be upgraded to support both single and
double density media dynamically.  However, this support must wait until a scheme
is worked out whereby the newer non-system handlers with double-precision block
arguments are supportable.  At that time, calls will be added for seemingly
"impossible" calls for blocks where the high-order block number is set to 7777.  The
handlers will always take the error return but will provide means for the
handler to return current mounting status and current device geometry to allow
file structure initialization appropriate to the current media, etc.

There is a semblance of support for the Control-S/Control-T routine; however, both
characters perform a current drive unit recalibrate [while certain diskette drives
can be ejected, the RX series does not support this].

There is full support for a slurp loader including the system handler reload
mechanism.  The code takes advantage of the ability to switch over to image mode
during the process of loading the "file" causing the reload and finishes using an
image read for the rest of the last sector,  When the RX02/RX03 double-density
media variant is developed, this method will be put to better advantage as the
entire logical block resides on a single disk sector.

An incredibly foolish design concept was introduced in OS/278 Version 2 for the
DECmate II.  When using this system, the original rational concept of waiting for
the done flag at the initialization of any device handler was violated and
"reversed" into the presumption the flag is **CLEAR**!  This cannot be implemented
in general because on machines with front panels, it is impossible to maintain the
reversed convention.  As such, it is always possible P?S/8 may be started with the
flag in this alternate condition.  [Note: This notion is absurd and incompatible
with all other handlers for RX devices including user-written handlers for byte
mode transfers, etc.  There is no justification for it whatsoever; apparently
standards didn't mean anything to the "developers" of the flawed OS/278 V2 system.]

As such, the handler takes an agnostic position with regard to the initial state of
the done flag.  To preserve the proper convention, the handler will exit with the
done flag either raised or in the process of raising; any other program should
function properly as intended.  Consider this a warning to anyone otherwise
attempting to program the RX series of controllers on DECmates.  Only P?S/8 handles
the convention properly.  Fully conforming programs can depend upon the done flag
if relevant.

To simplify implementation of the stand-alone bootstrap portion of the slurp loader,
it is likely in the next release all system device handlers requiring an
extended-memory device extension [such as all devices on DECmates] will define a
secondary boot standard of restarting the system at 07600 in the highest field of
memory.  It is expected that as part of the overall system definition, only systems
capable of running in strictly 4K of memory will require the potential of a longer
bootstrap at 07600.  The requirement of extended memory is available in the system
kernel by reading a reserved bit.  Programs such as P?S/8 BIN can use this to
advantage.  This will also allow more of the slurp loader code to reside in the
block allocated for the slurp loader.  As such, the use of the extension for
purposes other than the system handler will be minimized.

For example, on DECmate systems, the code that starts at 07600 will be reduced to:

	CIF	70
	JMP	7600

[The highest field of memory where the handler extension resides can also be
determined by bits in the system kernel.]

The proposed file name for the double-density variant for RX02/RX03 support will be
PQSRX2.PAL.  Creation of this system variant will depend on insertion of the proper
media when the initial system generation is performed.

PQSDSU.PAL

This is the system handler complex for the DSD-240-8 and Western Dynex drive.  The
drive consists of a fixed lower section of 5.0 MB in a geometry analogous to the
RK05F [which is arranged in the RK controller as a pair of RK05 drives; this aspect
of the support is meaningless here].  The upper section consists of removable
cartridges that resemble the RL01 media, but are actually closer to the design of
the RK05.  The cartridge capacity is also 5.0 MB.  Hardware sectoring similar to the
RK05 is used, except that additionally, soft-sectoring is used to double the
effective number of sectors, thus each individual transfer is 128 words.  This
allows full compatibility with P?S/8 [and also an easily produced handler for OS/8].
The interface is quite elegant and is in some ways the forerunner of the MDC8 SCSI
controller.

P?S/8 can run in only 4K on this device [and OS/8 can run in 8K].  Performance is
faster than the RK05 both in terms of storage, seek time and data transfer rates.
Reliability is actually much higher than the RK05.  DEC did not make a product to
compete with this drive, although prototypes for the PDP-11 were studied in-house
using Diable drives compatible with Western Dynex.  Instead, DEC produced the
inferior RL drive series that many complain about both in the PDP-8 and PDP-11
worlds due to features that are needlessly challenging to real-time software
writers.  The DSD interface performs fully automatic seeking in a like manner to the
RK05 while the RL drives require software to participate in the seek operations with
attendant overhead.  All DEC devices for the PDP-8 produced from this point onward
were inferior, and in many cases, even worse than their PDP-11 counterparts.

Because the Western Dynex drive is partially removable, the entire disk can be
backed up to two removable drives using a roundabout technique of copying using the
lower disk as a buffer.  While admittedly slightly more effort than having two
drives, the speed of the device more than makes up for this; in all other aspects,
this can be considered a dual-drive system.

By convention, OS/8 was used for P?S/8 development and other tasks on the lower disk
while P?S/8 was used on the removable upper disk for daily operations.

As provided, the handler assembles a system for the upper drive.  Instead of an
actual file named PQSDSL.PAL for the lower section, the source code contains
conditional assembly that requires changing one line to create an equivalent system
for the lower drive.

Provided handler components are analogous to the RK8E handler due to similar
geometry.  The only configuration used is where each P?S/8 logical unit is the
same storage as how OS/8 is implemented on the RK8E controller addressing a pair
of drives.

In theory, DSD offered a multiple drive configuration, but as a practical matter,
few installations had more than the single drive, considered quite large at the time
compared to other DEC offerings.  As such, while P?S/8 can be easily modified to
mimic some of the other configurations, as a practical matter this represents the
best usage as considered at the time.  Clearly an alternative could be to boot to
the lower section expecting the presence of the upper section as logical units 4
through 7, etc.  If this is done, double-sized logical units can be attained as is
used in the RK8E P?S/8 where each cartrdige is a separate logical unit, etc.

Note: Due to its elegance, it is recommended to potential new designers that the
DSD-240-8 controller be used as a model; despite this [and likely out of pure
ignorance] many chose to instead emulate the inferior RK8E/RK05 design.]  Due to the
all DMA design, emulating the DSD-240-8 in software [such as the SIMH simulator]
would be less of an overall effort than what was needed to implement the RK8E
support.  In essence, the handler initiates a DMA operation directed to an area of
memory within the handler that is a command to the controller to perform such as a
diagnostic seek, track format, data read or data write for up to 4096 words by 128
word increments [specifying the number of sectors involved].  The done flag is
raised after the command completes and the handler then has to check for errors,
etc.  Since very little PDP-8 code is used, this is a very low overhead device
which makes the task of emulation quite easy, etc.

PQSLTA.PAL

This is the system handler for the PDP-12 TC12 LINCtape.  It is completely analogous
to the TC01/TC08 system in all ways except for the following:

a) LINCtapes by nature cannot transfer in reverse, thus use a simpler mark code
   system that is 12-bit oriented [LINCtapes predate DECtapes].  There are less
   inter-block zones and other non-data words; as such there are inherently more
   blocks on the same media, all other things considered.  Note: OS/8, when used as
   was distributed, foolishly sets the logical size of LINCtape identically to that
   of standard DECtapes.  This was simply not thought through and savvy users
   modify OS/8 PIP accordingly to use more realistic lengths, although unlike
   DECtape, there may be a few more semi-standard tape lengths in use.  For example,
   it is quite likely that, while DECtapes are available as much as 40% of the time
   capable of being formatted for 3300 octal blocks [instead of 2702], this is a
   size generally achievable on all LINCtapes in good condition.  Thus, in some
   installations that use both media, this represents an easy choice of format.

   However, nearly all the time 3400 octal blocks can be achieved and perhaps more.
   In "premium" tape length situations, as much as 4000 octal blocks can be achieved
   which is the largest size possible due to hardware limitations.  [Note: If a 4000
   block LINCtape is used, it may very occasionally require manual rewind to assist
   the hardware tape controller search the tape afterwards.  This is because some
   LINCtape formatting programs add "phantom" blocks after the highest block and
   before block 0000 to assist in the process and the controller can become
   "confused" momentarily.]  Clearly, there are few tape media that can achieve the
   4000 octal block size, and these tapes should be selected carefully.  However, it
   is not uncommon to achieve a tape size not much less than this maximum attainable
   goal.  Various modified versions of the DIAL "MARK12" program have been created
   over the years to express part of this issue.

   A related issue is the purely wasteful misfeature whereby the storage size of
   the tapes is erroneously set to 129 words per block.  In general, this is
   completely counterproductive and borne of ignorance and lack of communications
   between program designers from the LINC and PDP-8 world.  Some important facts:

i)   With the exception of the Disk Monitor System on DECtape, no software exists
     where the presence of the 129th word on a DECtape block is necessary in the
     logical sense; this is otherwise merely a hardware restriction because all
     DECtape block sizes must be multiples of 18-bit words.

ii)  The 129 word format when used on LINCtapes is totally pointless unless by
     total contrivance, a user wants to backup Disk Monitor System DECtapes on
     LINCtapes only to eventually transfer back to DECtapes.

iii) The presence of the 129th word on LINCtape tends to limit the maximum number
     of blocks.  As such, all other things equal, a smaller tape size will be
     achieved for no good reason.  All tape transfers take longer as more tape must
     be traversed every time to move between the same logical blocks, etc.

iv)  A needless burden on the P?S/8 system device handler [and also the OS/8 system
     device handler] for TC12 LINCtape is a frill that presumes the tape might be
     129 words; as such, the word following the caller's buffer is saved before each
     block transfer, then restored afterwards.  By assuring the tapes are always 128
     words per block, this misfeature could be removed perhaps allowing for a better
     overall system handler.

v)   On the LINC-8, no program can handle 129 words per block unless expressly
     written for the format.  To do so means the standard 128 word tapes would be
     unreadable.  Since the code space for 128 word LINCtape handlers is at such a
     premium [that hardware modifications had to be made or the use of extended
     memory] the frill cannot possibly be supported.  The software must know ahead
     of time what to expect because the transfers are done by program transfer [and
     not DMA] and there is no interface within the controller to determine which
     tape type is in use.  [If a lot more memory space were available, it would be
     possible to try both formats when a handler first loaded, and then dynamically
     realize the proper size to use, it is still overly contrived as there is no
     way to know if a tape was dismounted.]  As such, the presence of this needless
     word is a major barrier to inter-system compatibility.

     As such, all sensible utilities for PDP-12 LINCtapes should only create 128
     words/block tapes.  Unfortunately, there are many tapes that were formatted
     using naive formatting programs; it is often a nuisance to ensure a tape is
     actually formatted without the superfluous word.  Many people resort to
     writing on LINCtape labels to indicate formatting date and exact block format
     and count to make this completely clear.  Additionally, many unrelated
     LINCtapes use multiples of 256 words/block to run one of the "standard" format
     software systems such as LAPx where x is a vague definition of system software
     series up to 6.  Note: LAP8 systems do not agree on the length of the tapes
     unless they are of the older variety associated with the classic LINC.  On
     that system, the tapes are 250 foot long and cannot address more than 1000
     octal blocks.

P?S/8 support for TC12 LINCtapes does support the superfluous 129th word, but does
not recommend its use for so many reasons.

b) On a 4K machine, the slurp loader cannot work as in all other systems because
   the tape controller is too automatic.  The slurp loader uses a buffer in
   07400-07577 to read the data.  If the tape uses the 129th word, the contents of
   07600 may be destroyed and replaced with a random value.  Thus, for this
   particular purpose, the use of 129 words/block LINCtape may actually corrupt the
   system.  As such, it is STRONGLY recommended that only 128 words/block LINCtapes
   are used; otherwise the system should be manually booted every time.

c) The slurp loader reload operation is supported which should restore the contents
   of 07600; this needs to be tested to ensure at least in this operating mode the
   129 words/block tapes cause no harm.

d) The use of the virtual loader will require write-enable on the system device, but
   will prevent this potential problem.  Additionally, this is the only way to load
   binary files that load into 07400-07577.  When the virtual loader is used, the
   129th word issue becomes moot.

   Note: Future releases of P?S/8 for TC12 LINCtape may implement workarounds for
   certain aspects of these problems if extended memory is available, perhaps as a
   part of the Logical Console Overlay support.

e) The Logical Console Overlay should support the ability to override the initial
   tape search direction which is hardware-controlled to always start in reverse.
   Using MTB [Move Towards Block] commands should gain better control over this
   issue; a table of last block transferred for each LINCtape unit can be maintained
   within the Overlay.

f) LINCtapes cannot be unloaded by Control-T; as such, both commands rewind the tape
   using MTB instructions.  This has never been researched in great detail; it is
   conceivable it can be accomplished within an extended-memory routine.

P?S/8 support for the PDP-12 includes modified copies of MARK12 originally taken
from LAP6-DIAL with changes to create more realistic tape formats, and a modified
version of the TC12F program to copy DECtapes to LINCtape format and vice-verse.

A current support problem is lack of source code for these two vital programs.
However, a surviving earlier release for TC12 LINCtape includes images of these two
programs; a stopgap measure would be to make copies of the two program images.

Source code was recovered for what tentatively could be a proper source file for
these two programs.  Eventually, the source code will be confirmed to match the
binary on the surviving tape.  Additional modifications will likely be needed to
better serve as proper utility programs on future system releases.

There is a logistic problem that can be solved by the implementation of the BINCON
program:

Creation of a complete P?S/8 system is accomplished using a kludged contrivance:
OS/8 BATCH is used to create a series of images of all P?S/8 programs on an OS/8
non-system device such as LTA1: [or DTA1:].  P?S/8 DUMP is a block-oriented patch
and transfer utility that takes command file input to automate the utility.  These
.DUMP files have been recovered from a working FLP system [see below] created
23-Feb-1988 as the original release of P?S/8 Version 8Z.

The basic flow of system creation is to first load all of the primary binaries into
memory and create a starter system.  [This process is basically system-independent
and in theory could be carried out by paper-tape binary loading; it has in fact
been done from existing P?S/8 systems.]  Conventions of the starter system respect
all limitations of all potential operating systems that could load the starter
system components.

Separately, the non-system media has to be created as specified so that all system
components that either are required for the creation of a system or are likely
options are created on the OS/8 non-system media in known blocks.

The .DUMP files have to be added to the starter system [presumably from a previous
P?S/8 accessible device with a proper TFS directory on it].  Once this is in place,
DUMP is executed with the proper .DUMP files to transfer the binary files to the
designated target blocks on the starter system.  This includes patching the system
programs directory directly to include the proper entry.

The OS/8 non-system media includes any and all of the utilities for all potential
P?S/8 systems supported, including the two critical components needed on TC12
LINCtape systems.  However, the binary files used require the use of PAL12, a
user-written program partially based on PAL8, or more properly stated, an early
and rather poor version of PAL8 before many patches were applied [ironically at my
request personally].

As a PDP-8 assembler, the early version of PAL8 is notably poor and obsolete, but
for the purpose of essentially being an equivalent for the purpose of adding on
LINC mode dual-assembly features, it was adequate to the task.  [Note: DIAL's
assembler, the standard assembler for LAP6-DIAL and DIAL-MS, is actually a poor
PDP-8 assembler lacking many features including literals.  As such, modifying the
earlier PAL8 produced a result adequate to these specific purposes.]

This assembler was sufficiently useful to create the binaries used on the recovered
earlier P?S/8 TC12 system, but it is not clear that there is a one-to-one
correspondance of the output of a proper assembly and the core-image files on the
recovered LINCtape.  Only a direct comparison can solve this issue.

However, there is another problem:

It appears that the development of PAL12 was stagnated and later taken up by others
with some "fanciful" notions about how LINC code is meant to be assembled.  No
version of PAL12 is known to exist in source form and to date, only an older
release of the earlier work exists; key language features are not present; this
should be treated as a "pre-release" version at best.  But it can assemble LINC mode
programming properly in general [but will still get assembly errors due to the use
of features that are missing in this earlier version.]

the later effort is known as PAL12-V5 and does support all of the features needed
in the assembly.  However, it gets **NUMEROUS** assembly errors traceable to the
fundamental misunderstanding of how to assemble symbolic LINC mode code!

It is theorized that when the newer V5 version was released, that many assumed it
was a clear replacement for the earlier work.  Unfortunately this is clearly not the
case; it may irrelevantly be based on a newer PAL8 internally, but the failure to
properly assemble all LINC code leads to the hundreds of erroneous error messages
when assembling these files with this "too-new" PAL12 release.

I started a small attempt to overcome the source-code problems using an acceptable
kludge that ought to not be needed, but it is quite tedious and there is still no
proper binary image available until the core-image binaries on the specific tape
mentioned above is made available.  [I require this to be functional on the SIMH
simulator.  There is no actual need for LINC hardware; the programs on the
recovered tape are known to work.  The images can be used for source comparison on
a purely PDP-8 system, etc.]

Admittedly, the program works by using obscure quirks of the DIAL assembler, but
because of slavish compatibility in P?S/8 PAL LINC mode assembly support, the
proper binary is produced [and no error messages!].

Thus, after the core-image files are recovered, the plan is to use P?S/8 PAL
exclusively to create proper source code for the source file collection, and then
use BINCON to produce binary files suitable for use with ABSLDR.  At that point,
the creation of the non-system device image can be completed.

To create the recent release of P?S/8 for TC01/TC08 Version 8Z, this has NOT been
the method used!  Instead, the recovered FLP system was used and appropriate blocks
of the additional programs were added to a recently assembled starter system for
TC01/TC08 as created on the PEPS for Windows version of SIMH.  There is also a
documented list of patched to Version 8Z to fix a small number of small bugs and
to maintain the P?S/8 DATE program.  [Note:  P?S/8 date handling is presently a
kludge; however, it can be maintained through 2099.  A set of patches must be
applied every 8 years; however, there is a three year "grace period" before the
patches must be applied.  As of Version 9A, the date format is being completely
revamped; the date will be completely supported for all dates from 01-Jan-1900
through 31-Dec-2411 with no need for maintainence whatsoever.]

Thus, in the short-term, additional P?S/8 releases will be derived primarily from
the TC01/TC08 version to maintain viability of all of the patches.  Future releases
will hopefully include the proper binaries for these two programs so that the
standard process of overall P?S/8 system creation will be restored.

Ironically, the ability to perform the original system creation may become available
again!  There exists exactly **ONE COPY** of OS/8 for the 8K modified LINC-8.  This
is because the LINC-8 cannot support more than 8K; hardware modifications to support
P?S/8 in 4K or OS/8 in 8K have only been applied to two machines to date, one is 4K
and the other is 8K.

On the unique LINCtape is a copy of OS/8 BUILD containing the handlers for this
system; the sources are presently lost; it is hoped they can be recovered from this
specific LINCtape [which is likely].  [It is conceivable the source code could be
recovered in the future as there are so many tapes to be recovered at this point!]

From a paper directory in the LINCtape cannister, it appears [and is likely] to be
the missing version of PAL12 that is the complete earlier release where all needed
LINC-oriented features are present and LINC code is properly assembled.

If this release can be recovered, the process of creating P?S/8 systems should be
able to be restored, etc.

In any case, this entire issue has been tabled until time permits, and certainly
after the BINCON program is developed.

Release of a complete P?S/8 Version 8Z for LINCtape can be more readily achieved,
but not until the core images are recovered to be included in the release.  A copy
of the release on DECtape can be created for potential transfer to a PDP-12 if an
existing copy of the TC12F program is already available [such as the original
program within a LAP6-DIAL/DIAL-MS LINCtape.  This method has been used in the past
effectively, etc.

PQSFLP.PAL

This system is for the HD 5.25" diskettes used on the MDC8 SCSI system.  The format
is a conceptual cousin of the standard PC-AT diskette format with 26 sectors per
track of 256 bytes/sector.  All MDC8 operations only use six of every eight bits
and are transferred via DMA two bytes at a time as 12-bit words.  Thus, this is an
ideal format for a PDP-8 device where all blocks are PDP-8 page sized.  The ease of
programming is astoundingly easy and a vague cousin of the DSD-240-8 controller
extended to support much larger hard disks and diskettes.  By convention, the
hardware logical devices on the MDC8 are 0 and 1 for hard disks and 2 or 3 for
diskettes.  Any hardware logical device can be booted, thus there is logical drive
0 and 1 as diskette drives.  [Note: This system is known as "FLP" and in OS/8 these
diskettes are known as FLP0: and FLP1:]

Each diskette holds slightly over the maximum capacity of the system device handler.
To aid in writing quick-and-dirty programs, the handler includes a logical device 2
or 3 to access the 40 octal blocks.  This should be removed when the non-system
device handlers are upgraded to use double-precision arguments.  At that point, the
non-system handlers can access blocks 00000 through 10037.  [Changes to MDC8
handlers are truly a trivial task.  The bootup for the entire device consists of a
few words where the hardware logical device select is part of the bootup which is a
skeletal SCSI command in memory, etc.]

A copy of this system was recovered apparently created on the inaugural day of P?S/8
Version 8Z [23-Feb-1988] complete with a lot of obvious files and a complete copy of
all of the standard .DUMP files used for any variant system component creation as
would be needed for any system.  [Note: P?S/8 standard systems always start with the
hardware-dependent system handler complex and some key utility programs in memory.
From the starter system, P?S/8 DUMP is run passed the appropriate .DUMP file to
transfer all of the common programs that should be a part of any system.  Additional
system programs may then be added optionally as needed; several .DUMP files are
available to assist in this final step.  On occasion, system programs are added
manually including updating the system programs directory; however, the process
delivers at least 90% of a complete system in a fully automated way [albeit a kludge
from an OS/8 non-system device where the images of the programs are in known
specific blocks on the OS/8 device].  Future system generation techniques will be
written to make this more sophisticated and less rigid.

The components beyond the starter system for the release of P?S/8 8Z [with minor
patches] for TC01/TC08 was created from this recovered diskette using the PEPS
package and SIMH from an earlier P?S/8 DECtape that was recovered and eventually
given to me.  While quite incompatible, I was able to use it to obtain the
first variant TC01/TC08 system which only partially functioned [because much of it
was in fact that older system!].  From that intermediary, the current system was
created.  It is hoped that the problems with the two PDP-12 LINCtape-oriented
programs is solved or at least properly contained [either by dummy placeholders] or
transferred binary by creating the BINCON program or perhaps recovery of the correct
PAL12 program] to attempt a complete system generation from only the source files.

A directory of what is on the recovered FLP system is available explaining the
purpose of all of the files present.  This was a fortunate recovery so the .DUMP
files did not have to be recreated.  Additional files include common binary patch
files to P?S/8 FOCAL to implement various function add-ons and Logical Console
Overlay files to achieve some of the more common custom modifications.  [Virtually
all of the files described above can be easily derived from the existing source
files as is intended by design.  A complete release should have available some
subset of these files to aid in customizng the system just otherwise finalized, etc.

PQSWNA.PAL*

The notation used on MDC8 systems is FLP for the diskettes and WNA for the hard
drives ["Winchester" disks].

The device handlers for P?S/8 and OS/8 were created in several development stages
over the course of several years as the project changed course several times.  In
the end, two variants were create using the same firmware.  One variant is based on
using 256 bytes/sector for all devices.  On these systems, all handlers for P?S/8
and OS/8 are quite elegant and can be written in a single page with extra room in
the handlers [depending on how many frills are added.]

The other variant was created for the sole purpose of supporting OMNI-8 because this
is the most powerful PDP-8-based system ever produced commercially.  A typical
OMNI-8 configuration is up to two large disks such as a Seagate ST-4096 with a
nominal capacity of 80 MB [the controller defines up to 416 MB disk support] and
the diskette drives used solely for backup.  Memory can be up to 512K using the
incompatible MEC8 controller.

When the MEC8 is in either of the extended memory modes, it is NOT compatible with
the PDP-8; as such no other software can use the drive; OMNI-8 is created as a
stand-alone system often typically loaded from an OS/8 system that is running on
another device [such as the RK8E/RK05].  [Note: P?S/8 for RK8E/RK05 could easily
maintain the source and binary files.]

The two incompatible modes of the MEC8 differ only in the hardware selection methods
of the extended memory.  In one configuration, KT8A compatiblilty exists from the
point of view of the actual memory modules.  [Note: CESI memory modules exist that
are 128K per board of static RAM.  This improves the performance over the DEC
dynamic memory, making all memory as fast as the 16K core stacks variant of memory
but much larger.]

Programming extended memory in either mode is not compatible with the instruction
set of the PDP-8.  The difference in the second mode is that only the CESI static
ram boards can be configured to allow up to four modules for a total of 512K RAM.

The programming is identically incompatible, but higher memory fields are allowed
to access the entire larger memory.

If the MEC8 is switched to compatibility mode, the machine is again made totally
compatible with the PDp-8 instruction set, but only the first 32K of memory is
accessable.  Programmers typically had to flip the edge switch on the MEC8 after
loading the first 32K of memory with the latest test release of OMNI-8, etc.

In this mode, the MDC8 is configured such that all sectors are 512 bytes/sector and
thus all transfers **MUST** be 256 words at the minimum.  It is most notable that
OS/8 CANNOT RUN on this configuration.  While not ever attempted, P?S/8 can be made
to run on this system by using the techniques found in the P?S/8 device handlers
for the RK8E.  OS/8 cannot be made to work here because OS/8 requires a means of
transferring only the first-half of a sector; without this feature, OS/8 crashes!
P?S/8 for the RK8E already has a technique for the writing of either half of any
sector; the only conceptual change is that even reading the first half of a sector
would require the use of the internal buffer which only complicates the code
slightly.  [Since MDC8 programming is inherently so easy, this postulation is highly
likely to succeed, but the needs of the programmers on the OMNI-8 project were such
that they were so expedient, they just used machines that had both MDC8 and RK8E.]

I am leaving out the "political" aspect of this device; there were many angry large
users of the MDC8.  I worked for one of the largest one and developed all of the
diagnostics and bad-block mapping and testing programs for the device.  The source
code for all of the project files has been recovered.

During an earlier phase of the economic lifetime of the MDC8, the large OEM
spearheaded all of the firmware development of the MDC8 because the device was
created because they needed many hundreds of units for their application.  The
early units included feeble software efforts that didn't work, which is where I
enterred the project and took over full responsibility for the PDP-8 software.

The first thing I pointed out is that it was predictable that OS/8 would crash; the
device did not implement the one/half sector transfer bit and the disk sectors were
512 bytes.  I explained to their firmware programmer what was needed, and we worked
out a mechanism that entirely solved the problem.

For the initial project I created diskette drivers that were programmed in an
analogous way to the RK8E [this project did not require a hard disk].  My
replacement OS/8 handlers were elegant and worked perfectly [after the firmware was
upgraded to support the needed feature.

I never wrote any P?S/8 support for this system, but clearly it could be done by
the techniques used in the P?S/8 RK8E system, etc.

The next project expanded this to support a different SCSI controller that became
the standard for the MDC8 with the support for the two diskettes and two hard disks.
The diskette programming became the same other than the larger size; all devices
were programmed in a like manner to the RK8E.  Again, I did not write P?S/8 for this
configuration, but it could always be accomplished, etc.

Some of these earlier files have been recovered, but all of this is moot.  A power
struggle ensued because CESI wanted to control everything, and as a result they
wrote new firmware in part to favor OMNI-8 perceived as a larger market as perhaps
the OEM I was working with had peaked.  Regardless, we had to work with what the
CESI firmware became.

They were unwilling to implement the one/half sector transfer bit, but instead we
were told to use 256 bytes/sector mode.  This became the standard and made it easily
possible to support P?S/8 and OS/8 on a more "level playing field" etc.

The first generation of this final configuration created diskette handlers for both
OS/8 and P?S/8 that are compatible [meaning that programs such as OS8CON can
function as intended.

I upgraded the hard disk software [including the diagnostic and maintenance utility
programs to handle the present system and we were quite successful with this
cofiguration.  [Ironically, while perhaps from CESI's vantage point selling MDC8 and
MEC8 and memory boards, OMNI-8 seemed a more "important" customer, more MDC8 boards
were sold to the OEM I worked with.]  [Note: At that point in time, most devices
could be configured for either 256 or 512 bytes/sector; however, eventually the 512
byte standard became far more dominant.  This limited choice of newer disks for the
MDC8 project.]

Varying hard disk drives were used in this project, especially the ST-4096 from
Seagate [and a few smaller drives for customers with more modest needs].  From a
software support standpoint, there was relative stability for a time.

Those familiar with hard disk-based DECmate systems are aware of the "umbrella"
system known as Master Menu.  Utilities exist to indirectly boot a multitude of
operating systems inherent in a system with larger storage.

I designed the rudiments of a conceptually similar system for the benefit of the
MDC8.  Areas are designated for the various data regions that have to be maintained
such as bad-block mapping tables, etc.

The systems defined above boot from logical block 0 of the disk, although in theory
stand-alone special-purpose boot programs can be defined to boot other operating
systems logically further up the disk, etc.

To implement MENU-8, the first track of the disk is reserved [32 blocks of code]
which can implement the beginning of a utility that locates additional modular
components by a lookup in a special-purpose small directory.  [Note: MENU-8 was
never finished, but enough of it was created to define the default secondary system
to be indirectly booted.

As such, first the PQSWNA.PAL system device handler was modified to support the
fixed offset to add onto all logical blocks for all purposes.  After this was
successful, the corresponding OS/8 handlers were also modified.  [I never got
around to writing the non-system handler, but as stated earlier in this document,
it is always much easier to write a non-system device handler once the system device
handler is complete.]

From PQSWNA.PAL, the corresponding OS/8 system and non-system handlers were upgraded
to support conditional assembly to allow the offset as an assembly option that can
be defeated [if MENU-8 support is not needed].

While the source code of PQSWNA.PAL is presently lost, this is not a serious problem
as all of the OS/8 handlers' source files have been recovered [along with the
diagnostic programs, which are actually stand-alone but honor a restart at 07600].

As such, it will be a straightforward task to recreate this source file from the
other source files, etc. [Note: All MDC8 source files bear resemblance to each
other; they all format a SCSI command in memory and then use IOT codes to have the
controller execute the command, etc.].

Should a viable MDC8 system emerge, any number of OS/8 and/or P?S/8 system images
can be created on it.  Future P?S/8 non-system handlers can access up to the entire
416 MB of the MDC8 storage if need be due to the use of double-precision block
arguments.  [The missing handler can establish a variable starting point, but can
only add on a single-precision offset to the base position chosen.]  All handlers
include conditional assembly to define various disk regions as required.  The unit
of measurement is the 1.0 MB of space a full-size P?S/8 system device handler can
address; OS/8 can address two adjacent ones at any one time.  P?S/8 non-system
handlers can be made to only address the designated regions if desired, but are
capable of addressing not only the entirety of a drive, but also a second hard drive
as well.

Note: An obscure ISA buss PC-compatible SCSI adaptor was created with firmware
compatible with the MDC8.  However, only the PDP-8 supports the disk mapping and
diagnostics to prepare the disk for use with the PC.

PQSL8X.PAL*

This is the P?S/8 system device handler, etc. for the LINC-8.  As in the case of the
RX01, 8K minimum is required for this system.

This is one of the system configurations that was never fully developed.  However,
there are surviving LINCtapes of older P?S/8 releases that use this configuration.

The source code was thought to be lost until very recently; the intention was to
derive the code exclusively from image dumps of the relevant blocks where the
system components are known to reside.  [Note: On older systems, the absolute blocks
where certain components reside vary, but with minimal effort, the specifics can
easily be derived.

These systems predate the support for the Logical Console Overlay, in part because
of limited access to the required hardware.  With the source code recovered, it is
a reasonable effort to write new code that can be totally compatible with the
present Overlay definitions.

Note: Due to unforeseen design problems, the LINC-8 can only support 8K of memory.
Additionally, the machine has to be slowed down and the memory wings retuned to
allow this.  No existent LINC-8 system can be upgraded past 8K unless a major
redesign of the extended memory bus is created firm up marginal timing.  In
particular, at least one original LINC-8 system owner contracted with DEC Computer
Special Systems [CSS] for the purpose of performing an upgrade to allow OS/8 to
[somehow] run on this 8K system.  Only an indirect solution was obtained by special
buss modifications to allow a single data break device to share memory access with
the LINC CPU.  [Note: The LINC-8 does not output a set of DMA cables.  Instead, the
data break facility is wired directly to the LINC CPU.  The DEC CSS modifications
allow only one DMA device; a DM01 multiplexor is not allowed.]  The configuration
chosen had to be the DF32 with the disk transferred slowed down using interleaved
disk formatting [as is used on the PDP-8/S].  Due to the cost, only a single DF32
platter was added [requiring 24/7 power] and no additional cabinets were added to
allow additional DS32 expander disks.  As such, much of the utility of OS/8 is
lost [there simply isn't enough disk space for anything but a limited subset of
OS/8; all commands must use the awkward form involving the use of non-system
LINCtape handlers as virtually all OS/8 programs must be located elsewhere.  As
such, the inherent speed of the DF32 is mostly wasted and the OS/8 system is more
cumbersome to use, etc.].

Recently, a copy of PQSMON.OLD was recovered.  Several "cast off" system
configurations are defined in this file [that predates the release of P?S/8 Version
8Z by five years].  The LINC-8 system device components are available within this
file and will be used as the starting point for recovery of this configuration.
[Final resolution of this matter will be determined when the contents of the
corresponding blocks on the recovered bootable LINCtape are compared to the source
code.]

If all goes as expected, the only missing components would be the never-written
interception routines for use with the Logical Console Overlay.  Final disposition
of this system depends on the availability of a working LINC-8 with extended
memory.

Other issues:

A working LINCtape formatter was obtained from an obscure copy of CPS-4K, a
partially functional system for the LINC-8 that can run on a 4K LINC-8 with some
serious restrictions that affect reliability: Certain portions of programs are
loaded into memory without any parity check of the data.  Additionally, the rest
of this system ironically suffers from many of the restrictions of the original
R-L Monitor system, namely the requirement that all system programs include embedded
LINCtape subroutines to access the system device[s].  [Note: CPS-4K requires two
LINCtapes, of which one is meant to be booted while the other is not.]

There is no source code for this routine, presumably derived from studying the logic
of how LINC systems interact with PROGOFOP as there is no direct capability present
to format the tapes.  [And no support for 128 words/block LINCtapes.]  This program
is completely functional; it needs to be disassembled and may lead to making some
incidental changes to be more compatible with the newest definition of a conforming
P?S/8 system program.

Some LINC-8 systems have hardware modifications for a variety of purposes.  One
major subset of what is known to have been performed allows the ability to read and
write DECtapes using software written by Charles Lasner which has been recovered.

In general, most of the modified machines were also additionally modified to allow
viable handlers to allow P?S/8 to run on a 4K LINC-8 [or OS/8 to run on a similar
system with 8K].

The software has been modified over the years and now includes both requirements
of hardware modifications to perform all functions.  With some effort, only a
dependence on the DECtape-oriented modifications could be achieved if desired.  A
surviving source file uses this lesser requirement, but the overall software is
not as well developed as later programs.  [Note: P?S/8 BLKCPY is the latest variant
of these programs; creating the DECtape support in the form of a P?S/8 non-system
handler allows the functionality using the best overall utility program to use for
the purpose.]

In particular, a surviving source file consists of a callable subroutine to read
or write 200 octal words/block LINCtapes, 400 octal words/block LINCtapes, 200 or
201 octal words/block DECtapes [transferred forwards or backwards] and 600
words/block DECtapes [transferred forwards or backwards].

PQSL8M.PAL*

This system is similar to the PQSL8X system as described above.  The differences are
as follows:

a) This system runs on a 4K LINC-8,

b) Additional hardware modifications are required that were created as an
   augmentation of the modifications made to support DECtape reading and writing as
   described above.  [In theory, the changes could be separated, but most of the
   modifications are common to both sections.]

The path to recovery is identical in all aspects to the PQSL8X system as described
above, including the need to write new intercept routines for the Logical Console
Overlay should 8K memory be available.

With all modifications in place, a much more practical OS/8 implementation can be
achieved [on 8K machines] than the much more expensive and cumbersome DEC CSS
modifications.  It is not known if both sets of hardware modifications interfere
with each other; LINCtape implementations on the LINC-8 are PDP-8 program transfer
peripherals and thus are likely non-conflicting.

Other peripherals.

PQSMON.OLD describes some level of support for a Sykes 7000 series diskette system.
It is likely this code was never seriously tested due to lack of available hardware
configurations.  It would appear the device is fairly well designed; as such, the
estimated effort to achieve a viable system is a small-to-medium project.

Over the years, some experimental code was written for P?S/8 for the DF32; however,
due to the very small size, support is not very practical.  The design prinicples
of P?S/8 do not address seriously compromised storage size; with the maximum of
three DS32 expander disks, the overall size could support a modest system.  Testing
done by various P?S/8 members indicate that the slurp loader can be achieved for
this device.

The similar but far larger RF08 system can easily be supported by P?S/8, but this
was never accomplished due to lack of availability of the hardware.  Estimates of
effort to support this device tend to favor a rather trivial effort to implement
all of the required components.  However, due to the far faster DMA speed, it is
not possible to create a slurp loader for the RF08.  [Due to sheer speed, the
virtual loader would run quite rapidly on this system.]

Charles Lasner has had contact with the RK08/RK01 system.  This device has a
superiour interface to the RK8E, but to disks half the size.  It is doubtful if
many systems were ever created with more than a single RK01 drive.  Due to the
similarity to the RK05 in terms of physical record size, the techniques used to
support the RK8E would be used to achieve a similar result.

A dual-bus 12-bit wide peripheral that otherwise programs identically to the
device 03/04 console device was created as part of a class project.  This produced
the PQSIPB.PAL system support which is essentially complete.  The specification for
this system is straightforward and could readily be constructed.

Work is being researched with regard to the SerialDisk handler as initially written
by Kyle Owen.  In its present form, the techniques used for the RK8E would apply.
It is hoped the transfer record size can be modified to support 128 words/block
transfers which would allow P?S/8 to run on 4K systems.  Many individuals own
small PDP-8 systems that are essentially paper-tape systems.  By adding in a single
serial port, these can be upgraded to run P?S/8.

